<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Vitus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 303</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Vitus was an early Christian martyr from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, said to have died in the persecution under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>. His relics were brought from Saint-Denis to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> abbey of Corvey in 836, spreading his cult across central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and he is the patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>'s cathedral. The name St Vitus' Dance, for chorea, comes from the dancing once held before his images.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-vitus.jpg" alt="Saint Vitus" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BSonne · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Veit_G%C3%B6lsen_Pfarrkirche_Fenster02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vitus, sometimes rendered Guy or Guido, was a Christian martyr from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and the dates of his actual life are unknown. The earliest testimony to his veneration is a note in the Martyrologium Hieronymianum, found in its three most important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and so probably in their common exemplar of the fifth century. J. P. Kirsch held that this public veneration shows he was a historical martyr, though no historical accounts of him survive. Modestus and Crescentia were originally separate traditions that later became combined with his, and they are probably fictitious.</p><p>A purely fanciful account appeared in the sixth and seventh centuries. It makes Vitus the seven-year-old son of a senator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a>, who resisted his father's attempts to turn him from his faith and fled with his tutor Modestus and Modestus's wife Crescentia, his nanny. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he drove a demon from a son of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, but because he stayed Christian he and his tutors were tortured, and they died in Lucania.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the hagiography Vitus died in the Diocletianic Persecution in 303. His veneration spread rapidly in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. In 756 Abbot Fulrad is said to have brought his relics to the monastery of St-Denis, and in 836 Abbot Warin of Corvey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> transferred some to his abbey, from which the cult spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> and eastern and northern Germany. In 925 King Henry I of Germany gave the bones of one hand to Wenceslaus, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and this relic remains in the Saint Vitus Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> he was counted among the Fourteen Holy Helpers, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> his feast on 15 June was marked with dancing before his statue. The dancing gave its name to Saint Vitus' Dance, the neurological disorder Sydenham's chorea, and made him patron of dancers and entertainers. In Slavic lands his name, Sveti Vid, may have replaced the older worship of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svantevit">Svetovid</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> his feast, Vidovdan, is tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a>. He is shown as a young man with a palm leaf, in a cauldron of boiling tar and molten lead from which, according to the legend, he escaped unharmed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint Vitus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was intended to anchor Christianity in newly conquered Saxony, and in 836 it received the relics of <b>Saint Vitus</b> from Saint-Denis.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-abbey-of-corvey">Prince-Abbey of Corvey</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mazara del Vallo, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilicata">Basilicata</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.65,12.59&amp;year=293" aria-label="Mazara del Vallo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Saint Vitus"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5845 4301h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5845 4301h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.65,12.59&amp;year=293">What was here in 293 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
